ID: B3-Ch07a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch7_assassinate,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_bet,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_door,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_fingers,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_force,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_massage,0)
set(v_b3_ch7_tyrant,0)
 

The world is spinning on a crooked axis, and my skull feels like someone split the bone with an adze. This has to be the most wretched hangover of my life. What did I drink last night to end up like this?

Memory offers nothing. The moment I reach for a single detail from before I passed out, a swarm of small needles drives straight through my frontal lobe. Sleep is the only answer. Another hour or two on a flat surface and the world will right itself.

Just keep my eyes shut and sink back into the pillow. Do I have a pillow? Cold, uneven stone bites into the small of my back. Where did I collapse? It hardly matters. Once I sleep this off, I can piece the night together.

Except I cannot lie flat. Something has me by the collar, hauling my torso upright and refusing to let gravity do its job. Now the grip tightens, shaking me with frantic, rattling force. It wants me awake. I dig in, locking my jaw and keeping my eyelids clamped tight.

The shaking continues, relentless and rhythmic. The nerve of it. Someone is disturbing a critically ill man. It is time to give this mysterious force a piece of my mind. A few well-chosen words ought to send it packing so I can rest in peace.

choice("Tell the mysterious force that I need a few more minutes to rest.", B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch7_force = 0)
choice("Tell the mysterious force to go away and stop bothering me.", B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch7_force = 1)
choice("Tell the mysterious force that its face is ugly and nobody likes it.", B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch7_force = 2)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch7_force == 0) {
 "Give me a few more minutes," I groan at the phantom in the dark. "My head is coming apart. I need to lie down."

The shaking stops. Gentle hands ease my shoulders back down until my head rests against the stone floor.

Reason wins out. The force backs off, leaving me to my recovery.

I drift off almost immediately, but barely three minutes pass before hands haul me upright again. Light taps land against my right cheek. When I ignore them, small fingers firmly pry my eyelids apart.

"Just a few more minutes," I slur, batting uselessly at the air. "Just five minutes and I'll--"

My vision snaps into focus, and the phantom resolves into Leila. She is kneeling over me, thumbs propping my eyelids open, glowing blue letters already suspended in the air between us.

"I'm sorry, Barry, but I can't let you rest any more than this," the floating text reads. "The royal guards could be on our tail any minute. We need to move."

As the glowing letters register, the fog lifts and reality crashes back in.

This is no tavern hangover. The teleporter malfunctioned, tossing us across the underground, and the jarring magical backlash knocked me unconscious. And Leila's father—he was taken. She is right. Every wasted second puts distance between us and Reidzek. We have to find an exit.

I scramble to my feet and scan the dark cavern. The sudden motion sends a wave of vertigo crashing through my skull, and the cave floor tilts beneath my boots. Leila catches me before my knees give out, slipping under my right arm and bracing my weight across her shoulder.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_force == 1) {
 "Go away," I snap at the unseen intruder. "Stop shaking me! I'm trying to sleep!"

The shaking relents, replaced by light slaps across my right cheek. When I refuse to budge, small fingers take hold of my eyelids and pry them wide.

"Didn't you hear me?" I grumble. "I told you to leave me alone. What does a man have to do to get some peace around here--"

Light floods my vision, revealing Leila kneeling directly over me, her thumbs holding my eyes open while a glowing line of blue text forms in the air.

"Barry, do you recognize me?" she writes, her brow furrowed with concern. "Can you tell who I am?"

"Yes..." I mumble, blinking hard against the glow. "Yes, sorry. I was just--"

The words stall as the fog clears and the truth hits me.

This is no tavern hangover. The teleporter malfunctioned, throwing us into these caves, and the magical backlash knocked me out cold. And Leila's father—he was abducted right in front of us. Standing here groaning is a luxury we cannot afford. We have to move.

I push off the ground and try to gauge our surroundings. The moment my head turns, a vicious wave of vertigo hits me full force, robbing my legs of all stability. Leila catches me before I hit the stone, stepping to my right side and pulling my arm over her neck so I can lean into her.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_force == 2) {
 "Go away!" I bark at the unseen pest. "Your face is ugly and nobody likes you!"

The shaking stops instantly. A heartbeat later, two sets of small fingers clamp onto my cheeks and yank them outward with vindictive strength, stretching my mouth nearly ear to ear.

"Hey, cut it out!" I protest, the words coming out as a garbled mess through my distorted lips. "Just because you're ugly doesn't mean you have to take it out on me for telling the--"

I cut myself off the second my eyes open. Leila is hovering inches away, frowning deeply as she pinches both my cheeks with deliberate force.

"Oh..." I manage, the pieces clicking into place.

"Are you awake now, or should I keep pulling on these for a while longer, for good measure?" glowing blue letters float in the air between us, accompanied by a raised eyebrow.

"No, I'm... I'm awake," I say, rubbing my stinging cheeks as she lets go. "Sorry about that. Head's still scrambled, and--"

Mid-sentence, the full memory of the disaster comes rushing back.

This was never a hangover. The rebel teleporter threw us into these deep caves, and the magical backlash struck me unconscious. And Reidzek—the king's men hauled him away. We have no time to waste bantering in the dark. We need an escape route.

I stand up and turn to survey the cavern. The sudden turn sets the room spinning in violent circles, and my balance vanishes in an instant. Leila darts forward and catches me before I hit the floor, looping my right arm over her shoulder to keep me upright.

}
 "You shouldn't be making any sudden movements," Leila writes, the blue light steady in the gloom. "That will only make your headache worse. Try moving your head slowly, until you get accustomed to your nauseating sensation."

"This is ten times worse than the last time we landed here," I say, gritting my teeth against the throbbing inside my skull. "Feels like my brain is trying to crack through the bone."

"Yeah," Leila writes. "The headache is a lot stronger now than it was before. With how badly this teleportation went, I'm surprised that we're even alive. The teleporting device must have had some sort of failsafe mechanism implemented so that people's lives wouldn't be put at risk in case of a serious malfunction."

"Wait a second," I say, glancing down at her. "Are you dealing with the same headache right now?"

"If you're talking about the feeling of small daggers moving through my brain, then yes," she writes.

"You have a strange way of showing it," I say. "I can barely string three words together without wanting to fall over."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Intro3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Intro3)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Intro3
TEXT:

 "Don't get me wrong," Leila writes, "the pain in my head is excruciating, but it's nothing compared to what we used to experience on a daily basis at the Beacon of Hope. I guess that when you grow up in an environment like that, you get to build up a bit of tolerance to pain and nausea without realizing it. Come on, let's see if we can get you to take a few steps forward. I'm sure you'll feel better once you make a little progress."

I take a breath and try to put one foot in front of the other, leaning heavily on her shoulder for balance. Ten seconds in, the cave floor tilts violently beneath me, and bile rises in my throat. I slide down until my back hits the rough cavern wall.

"Not happening," I say, pressing the heels of my hands against my eyes. "You need to go on without me. I'm dead weight right now, and every minute counts for your father. Leave me here. I'll catch up once my skull stops splitting, I promise."

"Don't be stupid..." Leila writes, the letters sharp and close. "I'm not leaving you behind. If any of the royal guards teleport themselves here and find you in this state, they will kill you in a heartbeat. Give me a moment. I'll try to see if I can remember a technique that I learned from my father a while back. He used to do this for me sometimes, when the other lessathi from the Beacon weren't looking, and it got rid of some of the pain from my head. I think it worked like this."

She kneels in front of me on the cold stone, stepping close into my space. Her hands come up, warm and steady, her fingertips settling against my temples with deliberate, measured pressure. 

At first it is just the warmth of her skin against mine. Then something shifts beneath the surface. It is not raw physical force, but a subtle, focused pulse entering the ragged aura around my head. Leila's gaze is fixed, entirely calm, her breathing quiet in the narrow space between us. With small, rhythmic circles of her thumbs, she coaxes the jagged, violent static in my aura into a steady current. The sharp daggers behind my eyes blunt, dissolving into a manageable, dull hum. The sheer sensory strangeness of her touch leaves me completely still.

"That was incredible," I breathe, blinking as the tunnel stops tilting. "Felt like real magic. Or an aura technique, like what the guard captain used."

"Even if I can't cast spells," Leila writes, rising to her feet, "I'm still pretty good at sensing the flow of auras, so as long as I can find the right pressure points, I can eventually figure out what I need to do."

She extends a hand and helps me pull myself up.

"How are you feeling?" she writes. "Do you think you can walk?"

"Much better," I say, testing my footing. "Still nauseous, but the daggers are gone. What about you, though? Is your head still pounding? Can you use that on yourself?"

"I've tried it in the past, but it only works if you're using it on someone else," Leila writes. "The pain is still there, but... I can manage. Anyway, let's see if we can trace back our steps from yesterday. I think we need to go this way first. Are you coming?"

choice("Ask Leila if she can teach me the massage technique, so I can use it on her.", B3-Ch07a-Massage, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Massage, v_b3_ch7_massage = 1)
choice("Let Leila handle her headache by herself and go follow her.", B3-Ch07a-Massage, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Massage, v_b3_ch7_massage = 0)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Massage
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch7_massage,2) if (v_b3_ch7_massage == 1 && v_magical_sense >= 2)
#if(v_magical_sense >= 2 && v_b3_ch7_massage != 0 || v_b3_ch7_massage == 1) {
 

"Before we move, show me how you did that," I say. "Let me try it on you."

"Barry, that's not-- it's not that simple..." Leila writes, stepping back slightly. "It would take too much time to explain it to you!"

"Give me the quick summary and let me try," I say. "If I can't get it right away, we drop it and go."

"Fine," she writes, relenting. "But it's going to be a VERY short explanation. Come here."

I step close and rest my fingertips against her temples, listening as she writes out the specific aura knots and subtle flow lines I need to track.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_massage == 0) {
 "Let's move," I say, falling in behind her as we head deeper into the tunnel.

"So... what do you think happened to everyone at the rebel base?" Leila writes as we navigate the rough corridor. "You don't suppose any of them got buried under the rubble from all those explosions, do you?..."

"The rebels and civilians might be in trouble, but our group has walked away from far worse than falling ceiling tiles," I say. "A cave-in won't finish them. The real problem is what comes next. If Peter was telling the truth, the king marched an army straight into the underground, backed by whatever royal guards and trainees he has left. It's an open slaughter down there."

"Can you try calling them through the transceiver?" Leila writes. "Maybe we'll get lucky and you'll be able to reach them."

"These cavern walls are packed with seredium, so don't hold your breath, but I'll try," I say.

I pull out the transceiver, tune it to Daren's frequency, and tap the activation rune.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_massage == 1 && v_magical_sense < 2) {
 For thirty seconds I press my fingers against the sides of her head, shifting angles and hunting blindly for the minute aura ripples she described. Nothing gives. The energy under my palms remains a tangled, stubborn mess, completely unresponsive to my clumsy pressure.

"You were right," I say, pulling my hands back with a sigh. "I don't have the touch for it. We're wasting time."

"Okay, let’s get going," Leila writes, turning to lead the way into the corridor.

}
#if(v_magical_sense >= 2 && v_b3_ch7_massage != 0) {
 At first my hands are clumsy, pressing too hard against bone. But within twenty seconds, my magical sense catches the faint, vibrating currents pulsing beneath her skin. I adjust my grip, softening the contact and steering the jagged eddies in her aura back into alignment, mimicking the exact rhythm she used on me. The tightness across her brow slackens, and her shoulders drop.

"The pain... it's going away!" Leila writes, her eyes widening as I ease the pressure. "I didn't think you could learn to do this so quickly! It took me hours of practice to get this good with it when my father first taught the technique to me."

"My enhanced magical sense helps with the guesswork," I say, keeping my thumbs steady until the last turbulent ripple smooths out. "Once you can feel where the aura snags, it's just a matter of guiding it. Tell me when the ache breaks."

"I think I'm fine, now," Leila writes, letting out a quiet breath. "There is still some nausea left, like in your case, but that can't be solved with a massage. We'll have to wait for it to pass on its own. Let's get going."

"Lead the way," I say, lowering my hands. "I think I have our route from yesterday mapped out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_massage == 2 && v_magical_sense >= 2 || v_b3_ch7_massage == 1) {
 "So... what do you think happened to everyone at the rebel base?" Leila writes as we navigate the winding path. "You don't suppose any of them got buried under the rubble from all those explosions, do you?..."

"The rebels and civilians might be in trouble, but our group has walked away from far worse than falling ceiling tiles," I say. "A cave-in won't finish them. The real problem is what comes next. If Peter was telling the truth, the king marched an army straight into the underground, backed by whatever royal guards and trainees he has left. It's an open slaughter down there."

"Can you try calling them through the transceiver?" Leila writes. "Maybe we'll get lucky and you'll be able to reach them."

"These cavern walls are packed with seredium, so don't hold your breath, but I'll try," I say.

I pull out the transceiver, tune it to Daren's frequency, and tap the activation rune.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Massage2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Massage2)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Massage2
TEXT:

 "Daren, are you there?" I say into the device. "Daren, respond!"

Static hums back. Nothing else.

"Dead air," I say, snapping the transceiver shut and pocketing it. "We won't know their status until we reach the surface."

"And by out of here, do you mean inside that tower with all the people chained up in the basement?" Leila writes, casting a wary glance around the dark.

"We don't know any other route out of these depths," I say. "We could wander these caverns for days and die of thirst before finding another surface tunnel."

"Last time, we were able to get away from there because we had someone to teleport us out..." Leila writes. "This time, the rebels' teleporting engineer is no longer on our side, so the only way out of that tower will be through their heavily-guarded front door."

"I know," I say. "We don't have good options. Besides, if we want to retrieve your father, that tower is our primary target anyway. Hitting it while they think we're dead or lost gives us the drop on them."

"My father?!" Leila writes, stopping in her tracks. "What do you mean? Why would he be in there?"

"The king needs him to override the Overseer," I explain. "From what we saw yesterday, that tower was Bilibolbrathatastran's main workshop. If the king wants your father to replace Billy Bob, that laboratory is the first place they would drag him."

"That's certainly possible, but it's also just as possible that they're simply keeping him in a prison cell until he agrees to cooperate with them," Leila writes. "There's no way to know for sure."

"The tower guards will tell us everything once we ask politely," I say. "Assuming they know anything useful."

"What I'm more interested to know right now is which way are we supposed to go next?" Leila writes, pointing toward a fork in the dark. "Left or right? All these tunnels look completely identical... I'm having a really hard time remembering which of these corridors we went through yesterday."

"This way," I say, gesturing down the left passage. "We'll know we're on course if we don't trip any wards. The traps we disabled yesterday should still be clear."

"Unless someone placed some new traps while we were away..." Leila writes.

"In a single night?" I say. "Doubtful. There weren't even regular patrols down here. The king didn't know these side tunnels existed. As long as royal guards don't drop on our heads, we have a clear run. Come on."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Massage3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Massage3)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Massage3
TEXT:

 We make our way through the winding subterranean corridors, navigating by subtle mineral variations along the floor and walls. Flower and Melindra's grueling rock-identification drills from last night's watch are finally paying dividends; without them, these uniform passages would be impossible to tell apart. The luminescent blue crystals jutting from the rock cast a cold, steady glow, sparing us the need to drain our stat devices for light.

"So... why do you suppose we were teleported here, of all places?" Leila writes as we inspect the rock faces to verify our heading. "Do you think there's any chance that the Barry from the teleporter room was secretly still on our side and wanted to help us escape?"

"Not a chance," I say. "This cavern is almost certainly the device's default dump coordinate when a jump fails catastrophically. Like you said, there has to be an emergency buffer to keep botched spells from depositing people inside solid rock. We ended up in this exact spot during the previous teleporter mishap. It's a built-in failsafe."

"Do you have any idea why they would want to teleport us out of the rebel base, instead of just leaving us there?" Leila writes. "Could it have something to do with my father?"

#if(v_b3_ch6_transceivers != 2) {
 "Likely," I say. "The teleporter operator was their inside man, so they knew you were Reidzek's daughter. The plan was probably to hold you as leverage to make him work. My inclusion was just bad luck—you were holding onto me when the spell triggered."

"I see," Leila writes. "In that case, it's probably just a matter of time until they find out where we are and send some guards after us. Maybe we should try to quicken our pace a little."

A few minutes later, we pass the alcove where Breynan and Illuna disabled the primary ward yesterday. We keep moving, straining our magical senses for any trace of pursuit. The corridor ahead remains quiet, but the heavy seredium veins running through the rock make distant detection nearly impossible.

If pursuers are masking their auras, we won't sense them until they're on top of us—and the same rule applies in reverse. We've kept our own auras clamped tight since waking, so tracking us by magical signature will be just as difficult for them. It comes down to who navigates this labyrinth faster.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_transceivers == 2) {
 "Yes," I say. "I caught Gontrok speaking to the teleporter operator before the jump. The king wanted you in chains as a hostage to force your father's cooperation. I only got dragged along because you had a grip on my arm when the gate fired."

"I see," Leila writes. "In that case, it's probably just a matter of time until they find out where we are and send some guards after us. Maybe we should try to quicken our pace a little."

A few minutes later, we pass the alcove where Breynan and Illuna disabled the primary ward yesterday. We keep moving, straining our magical senses for any trace of pursuit. The corridor ahead remains quiet, but the heavy seredium veins running through the rock make distant detection nearly impossible.

If pursuers are masking their auras, we won't sense them until they're on top of us—and the same rule applies in reverse. We've kept our own auras clamped tight since waking, so tracking us by magical signature will be just as difficult for them. It comes down to who navigates this labyrinth faster.

}
A dull, heavy shudder rattles through the stone ahead, followed by the distant crunch of shattering rock. Leila and I freeze, locking eyes in the dim blue light.

"That wasn't an explosion," I whisper. "Someone just put a fist through a solid wall."

"So it must have been either Hadrik... or Gontrok," Leila writes.

choice(""It was probably Hadrik."", B3-Ch07a-Hadrik, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Hadrik)
choice(""I think it was Gontrok."", B3-Ch07a-Gontrok, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Gontrok)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Hadrik
TEXT:

 "It was probably Hadrik," I say. "He and the others must have forced the teleporter operator to send them after us the moment we disappeared."

"Don't you think they would have tried to call us through the transceivers as soon as they got here?" Leila writes.

"They probably did, hit a wall of seredium, and decided to punch through the bedrock to clear a signal path," I say. "Let me try Hadrik's frequency again."

"If you say so..." Leila writes, looking thoroughly unconvinced.

I pull out the device, dial Hadrik, and tap the rune. Static hiss answers.

"Hadrik, do you copy?" I shout down the dark corridor, pocketing the metal disc. "We're over here--mmghf!"

Leila lunges forward, clamping both hands across my mouth with desperate force.

"Barry, what do you think you're doing?!" she writes, holding me clamped tight. "What if that's Gontrok out there and not Hadrik?"

"Oh, I hear you, little man!" Gontrok's booming voice echoes through the stone from down the passage. "Stay right where you are! I'll be there as soon as I clear out a few more tons of rock."

"My mistake," I mutter as the muffled crack of another crumbling wall reverberates through the floor. "Sorry."

"...Normally he shouldn't be able to pinpoint our location just from hearing us once," Leila writes, easing her grip. "Let's try to move further away from here. Hopefully, that will make him lose our tracks."

"Agreed," I say.

We push ahead toward the seredium tower. Behind us, the violent crashing continues at erratic intervals, echoing from disparate angles. He isn't tracking our footsteps; he is smashing rock at random. Maybe he expects that tearing down enough seredium will let his magical sense latch onto our auras. Before I can work out the giant's logic, his bellow rings out again, directed at someone else entirely.

"Are you happy now, you useless illusionist?!" Gontrok roars. "Did I smash enough stone for your miserable spell, or do I need to bring down the whole ceiling?!"

The cavern corridors flare with harsh emerald radiance. As the flash dies, a ribbon of luminescent green smoke hovers in the dead air, tracing the exact path we took from the teleporter chamber.

"So, I just need to follow this trail of smoke, right?" Gontrok's laughter rumbles through the rock. "Well, then! Sorry to have kept you waiting, friends of Hadrik. I'll be there with you shortly!"

"Damn it," I say, drawing my blade. "The smoke has our scent. Move!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Enchantments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Enchantments)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Gontrok
TEXT:

 "I think it was Gontrok," I say. "The teleporter operator must have pinned our coordinates and sent Gontrok through on our heels."

"Yeah, I also think that's the case," Leila writes. "We should probably just keep moving forward. Him smashing a few walls here and there shouldn't really bring him any closer to finding us if he doesn't even know which way we're going."

"Agreed," I say. "Keep moving."

A moment later, a booming roar echoes through the tunnels, putting an end to any doubt.

"Where are you hiding, you cowards?" Gontrok bellows. "I know you're in these caves! Show yourselves!"

We hurry toward the seredium tower while the giant continues to shatter stone in the distance. The impacts land erratically, with no clear direction. Maybe he thinks tearing through enough seredium will thin the rock enough to expose our auras. As I weigh the theory, Gontrok's voice echoes again, directed down a side passage.

"Are you happy now, you stupid illusionist?!" Gontrok snarls. "Did I finally clear enough space for you to be able to cast your lousy spell?"

Emerald light floods the corridors, blindingly bright. When it recedes, a hovering thread of green smoke snakes through the tunnel air, outlining the exact route we took from the drop point.

"So, I just need to follow this trail of smoke, right?" Gontrok shouts. "Well, then! Sorry to have kept you waiting, friends of Hadrik. I'll be there with you shortly!"

"Damn it," I say. "The spell is tracking our trail. Run!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Enchantments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Enchantments)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Enchantments
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_coward != 2) {
 We sprint down the passage, but the glowing smoke clings to our footsteps like a tether. As we round a sharp bend, a massive shape tears past us in a blur of dust and momentum, skidding to a halt to block the corridor.

"Well, well, if it isn't the silver-haired princess and her not-so-charming prince," Gontrok rumbles, dusting off his heavy shoulders. "I'd love to stay and chat, but the king wants the lessathi tinkerer's daughter delivered alive and in one piece. So let's strike a bargain. Hand her over, and I'll let you walk away with nothing worse than two shattered legs. Consider it a generous offer, given the alternative--"

Leila does not wait for him to finish. She crosses the gap in a fraction of a second, her daggers flashing toward his exposed throat. Gontrok tilts his head back just in time, but she pivots on the ball of her foot and drives the blades in again as he recovers. He ducks under two rapid slashes, scowling as the defense wears thin, and extends both massive hands to crush her in a bear hug.

Leila vaults backward out of reach, meeting his glare with cold resolve as blue letters ignite in the air between them.

"I thought you were supposed to bring me to the king 'alive and unharmed'," Leila writes. "Why are you fighting back now?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_coward == 2) {
 We sprint down the passage, but the glowing smoke clings to our footsteps like a tether. As we round a sharp bend, a massive shape tears past us in a blur of dust and momentum, skidding to a halt to block the corridor.

"Well, well, if it isn't the silver-haired princess and her smart-mouthed prince," Gontrok sneers, cracking his knuckles. "My orders are to bring the lessathi tinkerer's daughter to the king in one piece, but that can wait. That insolent whelp beside you called me a coward to my face during our last encounter, and I intend to take his teeth before I--"

Leila moves before he finishes the threat. She closes the distance instantly, daggers slashing straight for his windpipe. Gontrok flinches back just in time, but she stays on him, driving another strike as his boots hit the stone. After deflecting three rapid cuts, Gontrok tires of backing up and lunges forward with open hands to seize her.

Leila leaps clear of his reach, fixing him with a level stare as glowing blue text springs up between them.

"I thought you were supposed to bring me to the king 'alive and unharmed'," Leila writes. "Why are you fighting back now?"

}
"Quiet," Gontrok growls, scowling at the text. "The king never said I couldn't pin your arms. Besides, those swings were purely decorative. Taking a close look at your steel, neither you nor your little escort carry blades with enough enchantments to bite through giant hide, no matter how hard you swing."

"You really believe that you can accurately tell how many enchantments a dagger has on it just by looking at it?" Leila writes. "Don't you think this overconfidence of yours might end up costing you your life someday?"

"Hah!" Gontrok barks a laugh. "Someday, maybe. Not today. If you're so confident in your little toothpicks, let's make a wager. I'll stand right here for sixty seconds. You can try to slit my throat, sever my head, whatever you like. I won't raise a guard and I won't step away. But if I'm still on my feet when the minute ends, you surrender and walk to the king without a struggle. Deal?"

"You're going to regret this," Leila writes, her eyes narrowing as she brings her daggers to a guard stance.

Gontrok bares his teeth in a predatory grin.

"We have a deal, then!" he says.

choice("Attack Gontrok together with Leila, while he isn't defending himself.", B3-Ch07a-Attack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Attack, v_b3_ch7_bet = 0)
choice("Try to convince Gontrok that our weapons are in fact as sharp as Daren's sword.", B3-Ch07a-Convince, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Convince, v_b3_ch7_bet = 1)
choice("Try to find a way to defeat Gontrok without using any of our weapons.", B3-Ch07a-Weapons, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Weapons, v_b3_ch7_bet = 2)
choice("Tell Leila that she shouldn't take the bet and that we should fight Gontrok together instead.", B3-Ch07a-Provoke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Provoke, v_b3_ch7_bet = 3)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Attack
TEXT:

 "Whenever you're ready, princess," Gontrok says, planting his boots wide. "The count starts the second steel touches skin."

Leila nods once. She darts forward, daggers flashing in an X-cut across his throat. At the same moment, I circle behind him, driving my own dagger into the nape of his neck with all my weight behind it.

The blades hit like wood against iron. The giant's dense hide does not yield, deflecting both edges without giving up a single drop of blood.

"I don't recall inviting the flea to the party," Gontrok rumbles, glancing back over his shoulder at me.

"Who says I need an invitation?" I say, stabbing the point into the same spot again.

"Cheeky little bastard," he snorts. "I'll break that arm in three places once the clock runs out."

Leila changes her angle in a flash. Abandoning the throat, she drives both dagger tips straight toward his open eyes.

Gontrok flinches, violently throwing his upper body backward to save his vision.

"Hold on," he snaps, scowling down at her. "That violates the wager. I said throat cuts and decapitation, not taking my eyes."

"You said I could slit your throat, cut your head off or 'whatever'," Leila writes. "The 'whatever' part of your statement implies that I could also attack you in any other way I'd see fit."

"I meant whatever strike you wanted against my neck," Gontrok argues.

"Then maybe you should have been more specific with your phrasing," Leila writes.

"You went for my eyes!" Gontrok's voice rises in anger. "You broke the terms!"

"So did you, by moving away from your spot before the sixty seconds were up," Leila writes. "From my point of view, you are the one who lost the bet, not me."

"It appears that we are at an impasse, then," Gontrok says, his fists clenching tight.

"So it would seem," Leila writes.

"I wanted to handle this cleanly, but you leave me no choice," Gontrok says. "The king will accept you with broken wrists if it comes to it. The gloves are off. Defend yourself."

"Less talking. More fighting," Leila writes.

"As you wish," Gontrok says.

"Leila, on him together!" I call out.

She gives a sharp nod, and we square our stances as the giant shifts forward.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Respite, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Respite)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Convince
TEXT:

#if(v_bluff < 3) {
 "You realize Leila isn't bluffing, right?" I tell Gontrok, trying to inject some steel into my voice. "Our blades carry the exact same enchantments as Daren's broadsword."

"Did you honestly expect a pathetic lie like that to land?" Gontrok scoffs. "Save your breath. Come on, princess! The count starts the second you touch me."

Leila nods and drives both daggers toward his throat with blistering speed. The blades scrape against his thick hide with a dull rasp, failing to pierce even the outer layer of skin.

"Is that all?" Gontrok mocks. "I was hoping for something that would make me sweat. Total disappointment."

Leila adjusts instantly. Abandoning the throat, she drives the tips of both daggers straight toward his pupils. Gontrok panics, leaping backward to protect his vision.

"Hold on," Gontrok barks, his brow furrowing. "That wasn't part of the deal. I said throat cuts and severed heads, not blinding."

"You said I could slit your throat, cut your head off or 'whatever'," Leila writes. "The 'whatever' part of your statement implies that I could also attack you in any other way I'd see fit."

"I meant whatever strike you wanted against my neck," Gontrok insists.

"Then maybe you should have been more specific with your phrasing," Leila writes.

"You went for my eyes!" Gontrok snarls, his temper fraying. "You broke the terms!"

"So did you, by moving away from your spot before the sixty seconds were up," Leila writes. "From my point of view, you are the one who lost the bet, not me."

"It appears that we are at an impasse, then," Gontrok says, his eyes dark.

"So it would seem," Leila writes.

"I wanted to handle this cleanly, but you leave me no choice," Gontrok says. "The king will take you with a few broken bones if necessary. I'm done holding back. Prepare yourself."

"Less talking. More fighting," Leila writes.

"As you wish," Gontrok says.

"Leila, let's take him together!" I shout.

She nods, and we both drop into fighting stances as Gontrok shifts his weight.

}
#if(v_bluff >= 3) {
 "You do realize Leila wasn't bluffing, right?" I say, keeping my tone perfectly flat and matter-of-fact. "Our weapons carry the exact same edge enchantments as Daren's greatsword."

"Did you honestly think a barefaced lie like that would-- work on-- wait a minute, what did you say?..." Gontrok's sneer falters, his eyes darting between our blades.

"I said our daggers are carrying Daren's standard offensive runes," I tell him with complete boredom. "We share a camp with a master enchanter. Did you think he sent us into combat with dull kitchen knives?"

"No, that's absurd!" Gontrok stammers, taking a half-step back. "Runes of that grade take rare reagents... and hours of focus! He couldn't have--"

"Believe whatever helps you sleep," I shrug. "I just thought I'd give you fair warning. Have fun in the underworld explaining how you died standing perfectly still like a practice dummy."

"Gontrok, prepare yourself," Leila writes, raising her daggers in a tight grip. "I'm going to try slitting your throat first. Make sure that you don't move an inch."

"Hold on a second..." Gontrok says, sweat beading across his forehead. "Give me a moment to think this through--"

"Here I go," Leila writes, launching forward with both blades aimed straight for his jugular.

Panic overrides his pride. Gontrok leaps backward with a startled grunt, clearing the arc of her daggers by two feet. He freezes, staring at her with wide, wild eyes as the reality of his retreat sinks in.

"You broke the deal," Leila writes simply. "You weren't supposed to move from that spot before the sixty seconds were up. This means that I win and you'll leave us alone. Correct?"

"No!" Gontrok roars. "That wasn't fair! I told you I needed a second to think, and you charged anyway!"

"You asked for a delay after we both agreed to the terms," Leila writes. "There was no pause clause in the bet. Are you going back on your word?"

"No, of course not!" Gontrok snarls, his face flushing crimson. "A giant never breaks his oath!"

"So, you will let us go, then?" Leila writes, calm and steady.

"I-- my orders-- Argh! To the hells with it!" Gontrok spits on the stone. "A deal is a deal. I won't soil my honor over two conniving runts. Take your lives and get out of my sight before I change my mind!"

"What did you just say?!" a sharp, disembodied voice cuts through the corridor.

It is the illusionist royal guard from yesterday. His voice rings out from everywhere and nowhere at once, echoing off the stone walls while his aura remains completely suppressed.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Respite, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Respite) if (v_bluff < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Loyrang, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Loyrang) if (v_bluff >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Provoke
TEXT:

 "Leila, don't take the bait!" I say. "He's looking for an excuse to pin you. We fight him together. Two targets are harder to handle than one."

"Maybe you're right," Leila writes. "As convenient as his terms sound, there is no reason to trust a word he says."

"Are you accusing me of breaking an oath?" Gontrok rumbles, looking genuinely offended.

"We don't know you," Leila writes. "You're a mercenary working for a tyrant. Your word means nothing to us."

"Very well," Gontrok growls, cracking his neck. "I tried to offer you an easy road. The king will accept you with broken wrists if it comes to it. I'm done holding back. Prepare yourselves."

"Less talking. More fighting," Leila writes.

"As you wish," Gontrok says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Respite, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Respite)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Respite
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_agility < 4 || v_reflexes < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_agility >= 4 && v_reflexes >= 4)
#if(v_reflexes < 4) {
 The giant feints a step toward Leila, drawing her guard low, then pivots on his heel and lunges straight at me.

Before my muscles can even register the shift, his massive right hand clamps around my skull like an iron vice. He clenches his fist, and my world shatters into dark silence.

}
#if(v_reflexes >= 4 && v_agility < 4) {
 The giant feints toward Leila, then snaps his focus to me, closing the distance in a single bound.

My reflexes fire, and I throw myself to the side just under his reach. But without enough raw speed to break away, Gontrok is on me before my boots find purchase. His right fist follows through with crushing momentum, caving in my skull before I can bring up a guard.

}
#if(v_agility >= 4 && v_reflexes >= 4) {
 The giant feints toward Leila, then pivots instantly toward me, his boots tearing up stone as he lunges.

My reflexes snap into gear. I duck beneath his reaching arm, throwing my weight into a desperate sprint across the cavern floor. Even with my speed pushed to its physical limit, the giant stays glued to my heels, his heavy fists grazing my shoulders with every swing. All I can do is weave through the narrow stone passages, dodging every lethal strike by a fraction of an inch while searching for an opening.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_bet == 0 && v_is_dead == 0 || v_b3_ch7_bet == 1 && v_is_dead == 0) {
 While I draw his focus, Leila slips around his blind spot. Instead of swinging at his armored back, she cuts in from the flank, driving both dagger points straight toward his eyes. Gontrok jerks backward, throwing up a forearm to guard his face and breaking off his pursuit.

In that fleeting second of breathing room, my magical sense catches a strange ripple in Gontrok's aura. Something about his magical energy is jagged and erratic. Isolating the flaw would require three or four seconds of unbroken concentration—seconds where I would be completely defenseless. Taking the time could be suicidal.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_bet == 3 && v_is_dead == 0) {
 While I draw his focus, Leila slips around his blind spot. Instead of swinging at his armored back, she cuts in from the flank, driving both dagger points straight toward his eyes. Gontrok jerks backward, throwing up a forearm to guard his face and breaking off his pursuit.

In that fleeting second of breathing room, my magical sense catches a strange ripple in Gontrok's aura. Something about his magical energy is jagged and erratic. Isolating the flaw would require three or four seconds of unbroken concentration—seconds where I would be completely defenseless. Taking the time could be suicidal.

}
