"Think for ten seconds, Gontrok," Loyrang says, his tone hardening. "Your scheme was doomed from day one. You can't even have a private conversation without blabbing your coup to one of the king's most loyal subordinates. The king knew about your ambitions before you even finished unpacking. The only reason he kept you around was because he suspected the organizers of rigging the protection spell against Ollendor citizens, and he needed a heavy hitter to protect him from foreign participants. You're a disposable shield. The second the tournament concludes, he'll have you executed in your sleep."

"You don't know that!" Gontrok snarls.

"I know how rulers think," Loyrang counters calmly. "I saw his face every time you spoke to him. He doesn't trust you for a second. He's playing to your greed to keep you on a leash. That's why he made you captain. He knows what you want and feeds it to you to keep you tame. But the moment your usefulness runs out, you're getting put down."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Vibrating, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Vibrating)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Vibrating
TEXT:

 "I've heard enough!" Gontrok roars, his hands curling into massive fists. "You won't lay a finger on the king. I'll break you in half first."

"Is that a threat?" Loyrang asks, unbothered. "Gontrok, my friend... Did you ever wonder why I agreed to travel with you on day one of this tournament? Did you think I'd walk alongside anyone I thought had a prayer of beating me in a real fight? You really don't know me at all."

"First you insult my mind, and now you mock my strength?" Gontrok's face twists with rage. "You think you can posture like this just because your scimitars carry high-tier edge runes?"

"It's far more than sharp steel," Loyrang says, his fingers resting lightly on his pommels. "Test me whenever you're ready. Just don't whine when you lose an arm or two."

Gontrok lets out a harsh, ragged laugh. It is not the laugh of an amused man; it is the fractured sound of someone right on the edge of snapping.

"What a day," Gontrok rumbles, wiping spittle from his chin. "First I babysit a cowardly teleporter, then two children make a fool of me, and now my trusted comrade promises to butcher me in a ditch. And on top of it all, this miserable buzzing in the stone won't stop, and it's DRIVING ME OUT OF MY MIND!"

He isn't imagining it. The blue crystals studded along the cavern walls have been thrumming with a high, vibrating resonance that has steadily intensified over the last few minutes. Gontrok glares at the rock, waiting for the frequency to die down, but the vibration only grows harsher, rattling the loose gravel beneath our boots. He raises his voice to shout over the ringing tone.

"So, you want to show me how much stronger you are, Loyrang?!" Gontrok bellows. "Is that it? Or is this another trick? Did you plant a ward beneath my feet while I was talking? I know your kind! You non-giants are all identical! Not a shred of dignity among the lot of you! Does honor mean nothing to you people? Is there-- ARGH, will this thrice-damned noise shut up?! What is wrong with these rocks?! Can't you see we are in the middle of a conversation?! Silence! I said BE QUIET!!!"

Losing all control, Gontrok wheels around and hammers the side of his massive fist straight into a protruding blue crystal on the wall.

The stone does not crack. Instead, the crystal blazes with blinding, violent azure light that engulfs the corridor, searing our vision white.

When the brilliance recedes, an agonizing scream tears through the tunnel—a shriek of sheer, unraveling horror.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Fingers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Fingers)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Fingers
TEXT:

 "What is this?!" Gontrok screams, thrusting his left hand into our faces.

What I am looking at defies basic anatomy. His left hand is missing its ring finger, pinky, the corresponding knuckles, and the entire outer half of the palm. But there is no severed flesh, no pooling blood, not even a trace of scar tissue. The remaining three digits meet smooth, unbroken skin, as if his hand had been shaped that way in the womb.

Worse still is the flicker in my own memory. For a fraction of a second following the azure flash, my brain tried to rewrite reality—offering the casual suggestion that Gontrok had only ever possessed three fingers. Even with the clear memory of his intact hand fresh in my mind, the urge to accept the altered state was visceral.

"Why are you staring at me like that?!" Gontrok shouts, his eyes wide and wild. "Are you all blind?! Look at my hand! What happened to it?!"

"What do you mean, 'what happened'?" Loyrang asks, looking genuinely baffled. "It's your hand. Did that blow knock your memory loose?"

"Look at the digits, you fool!" Gontrok bellows. "There are only three!"

"Yes, there are three fingers," Loyrang says slowly. "You've had three fingers since the day I met you. Are you looking for sympathy? Do you want me to tie an arm behind my back to make it fair?"

"Stop playing games!" Gontrok's voice cracks with panic. "The two fingers vanished the second I struck that crystal! You were standing right here! You saw it!"

"Gontrok, pull yourself together!" the illusionist's voice snaps from the empty air. "The traitor must have slipped a mental illusion past us during the flash. Listen to me: you were born with three fingers on that hand. Don't let a cheap parlour trick rattle you!"

As the guards argue, Leila catches my sleeve, pulling me slightly behind a stone outcropping. Her eyes are wide, her jaw tight.

"Barry... please tell me that I'm not going crazy," she writes, the blue letters trembling faintly. "You also remember Gontrok having five fingers on his left hand before, right? It's not just some mind affecting spell. Those fingers really existed... right?"

choice(""You're not going crazy. His left hand definitely had five fingers before."", B3-Ch07a-Disappeared, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Disappeared, v_b3_ch7_fingers = 0)
choice(""What are you talking about? His left hand has always had three fingers."", B3-Ch07a-Disappeared, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Disappeared, v_b3_ch7_fingers = 1)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Disappeared
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch7_fingers == 1 && v_bluff >= 1) {
 "What are you talking about?" I whisper while the royal guards shout amongst themselves. "His left hand has always had three fingers."

"Barry..." Leila writes, her eyes narrowing. "You already knew I would be able to tell that you were lying before you said that, didn't you?"

"I figured, yeah," I admit.

She delivers a sharp punch to my bicep.

"Well, I suppose I am at least grateful that you didn't see the need to use your bluff stat on me to fool me for real," Leila writes. "That would have meant taking the joke a little too far."

"Even I have limits," I say, rubbing my arm.

"Right..." she writes, though the tension around her shoulders softens slightly. "So, do you have any idea what might have happened after Gontrok hit that crystal?"

"The fingers closest to the impact vanished," I say. "The crystals carry an automated defense against direct physical trauma. What turns my stomach is the complete lack of trauma. No bleeding, no scar, and for a split second my own mind tried to believe he was born that way. The fingers weren't amputated. They were erased from history."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_fingers == 1 && v_bluff == 0) {
 "What are you talking about?" I whisper while the royal guards shout amongst themselves. "His left hand has always had three fingers."

"Barry..." Leila writes, giving me an unamused look. "You already knew I would be able to tell that you were lying before you said that, didn't you?"

"I figured, yeah," I say.

She lands a solid punch on my shoulder.

"Is it okay if I interpret this as another one of your misguided attempts to lighten the mood?" she writes.

"It broke your panic, didn't it?" I point out. "You're glaring at me instead of shaking."

"I suppose..." she writes, taking a slow breath. "So, do you have any idea what might have happened after Gontrok hit that crystal?"

"The fingers closest to the impact vanished," I say. "The crystals carry an automated defense against direct physical trauma. What turns my stomach is the complete lack of trauma. No bleeding, no scar, and for a split second my own mind tried to believe he was born that way. The fingers weren't amputated. They were erased from history."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_fingers == 0) {
 "You're not going crazy," I whisper, keeping my voice low while the guards argue. "His left hand definitely had five fingers before."

"Thank the gods..." Leila writes, letting out a ragged breath. "I thought my own head was unraveling. So, do you have any idea what might have happened after Gontrok hit that crystal?"

"The fingers closest to the impact vanished," I say. "The crystals carry an automated defense against direct physical trauma. What turns my stomach is the complete lack of trauma. No bleeding, no scar, and for a split second my own mind tried to believe he was born that way. The fingers weren't amputated. They were erased from history."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_fingers == 0 || v_bluff == 0 || v_bluff >= 1) {
 A cold dread settles over my chest, prickling down my spine. The Magium is the only entity in existence capable of pruning reality and retroactively editing memories. Are these crystals tethered directly to the Magium? Is it observing us through the stone?

I look around at the walls. Blue mineral veins run through every corridor, thousands of glowing facets glittering in the gloom. If the Magium perceives through these formations, there is no shadow in these caverns deep enough to hide in. We are trapped in a glass cage.

"Barry, what's wrong?" Leila writes, stepping into my field of vision. "Hey, look at me."

My survival instinct has always been broken. Physical death never frightened me—a clean blade or a burst of flame is just an end. But nonexistence is different. The thought of being cast back into that endless, empty void, floating in timeless contradiction where you both exist and do not exist forever... my hands are shaking. I cannot go back to that place.

Down the tunnel, Gontrok's bellow cuts through my spiraling thoughts.

"I've had it with this nonsense!" Gontrok roars at Loyrang. "If you refuse to believe your own eyes, I'll prove it on this rock!"

He stoops, scooping up a jagged chunk of basalt in his right hand.

"I will smash this stone into that crystal," Gontrok snarls, raising his arm, "and when it ceases to exist, we'll see who belongs in an asylum!"

"Don't!" I yell, stepping forward.

Gontrok's arm freezes mid-swing. His entire frame locks rigid, arrested by an unseen, crushing weight. While the giant strains against the invisible binding, air ripples behind him, and Eiden drifts into view, hands resting casually in his sleepwear pockets, his eyes shut as always.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Barbarian, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Barbarian)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Barbarian
TEXT:

 "Leave it to a glorified barbarian to conclude that smashing a rock into a crystal that just deleted his fingers is a sound idea," Eiden says in a light, conversational tone. "Tell me, Gontrok... How does it feel knowing the only practical function of your skull is preventing rainwater from pooling in your throat?"

The moment Eiden appears, the hum in the cavern walls spikes. Every crystal within twenty yards begins vibrating violently, the ringing tone rising in pitch. They are reacting to him. But why? Melindra is a stillwater, and the crystals never stirred around her. 

Arraka's passing comment surfaces in my memory: Eiden and the golden fox learned their primordial magic from the Magium itself. If Eiden maintains a direct link to the Magium, it explains the resonance—but what are these crystals made of to respond to him like this?

"So it was you," Gontrok grunts, straining his neck to catch Eiden in his peripheral vision. "The crystals were humming for you. You're behind this."

"A breathtaking deduction," Eiden says, gliding a step closer. "Now tell me, Gontrok: who ordered the demolition of the underground rebel sanctuary, which happens to be lined with these very same blue crystals? Was it your initiative, or the king's?"

"The king gave the order, but I had the pleasure of carrying it out," Gontrok rumbles, a vicious grin breaking across his face. "Your precious rebels are buried under forty feet of collapsed stone by now. And so are your little intruder friends. I knew you were sheltering them. But now they're crushed, except for these two runts. How does it feel, Creator? Are you finally going to drop the act and try to avenge them?"

"Your garrison is already dead," Eiden replies calmly. "The rebels, the underground king, and the intruders dismantled them in minutes, and they are currently mopping up the reinforcements."

"Liar!" Gontrok shouts, his arm still locked above his head. "You're bluffing!"

"Listen carefully, Gontrok," Eiden's voice turns icy. With a subtle flick of his aura, he twists Gontrok's massive frame around to face him, leaving the giant's arm pinned overhead in humiliating stillness. "Your belief is entirely irrelevant to me. You remain breathing solely because I require an answer. Has the king ever spoken to you regarding the third golden rule?"

"Golden rule?" Gontrok spits. "What drivel are you spouting? What is with you humans and your pathetic parlor tricks? Is this your idea of combat? Holding me helpless while you line up a killing blow? Where is your honor?"

"Honor?" Eiden's brow arches in genuine bewilderment. "Forgive me, but are you not the very same Gontrok celebrated for slaughtering the largest number of dwarven civilians during the last Northern war?..."

"So what?" Gontrok sneers. "What does that have to do with anything?"

"Ah," Eiden murmurs softly. "I see..."

He drops the aura lock. Gontrok's arm falls, the basalt stone clattering onto the floor.

"You may attack now," Eiden says pleasantly. "I will not employ a single spell against you. Enjoy your honorable duel."

"I don't care what madness drives you," Gontrok snarls, "you're going to choke on it."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Honorable, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Honorable)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Honorable
TEXT:

 Gontrok lunges with explosive speed, throwing a rapid flurry of heavy punches aimed to crush Eiden where he stands.

Eiden does not flinch. Slipping the massive fists with effortless, microscopic head movements—the exact evasive pattern he used against Daren on day one—he ducks beneath an overhand hook, snatches Gontrok's wrist, and twists the giant's arm behind his back. In a blur of motion, his fingertips strike three distinct pressure points along the elbow joint.

Eiden plants his open palm against the outside of the joint and pushes.

The forearm snaps backward with a sickening, wet crack, the bone splintering outward against the skin. Gontrok lets out a strangled howl of agony, staggering sideways as his ruined limb dangles limp.

"What did you do?!" Gontrok wheezes, sweat pouring into his eyes.

"I granted you an honorable duel," Eiden says mildly, his eyes still closed. "Was that not your request?"

"Rot in hell, you sadistic monster!" Gontrok roars, driving his three-fingered left fist straight at Eiden's jaw.

Eiden catches the incoming knuckles in his palm. Without shifting his footing, he bends the giant's remaining fingers backward until the joints snap like dry twigs. As Gontrok shrieks again, Eiden places his other hand flat against the giant's forehead.

The skull detonates.

A spray of bone, brain matter, and blood coats the cavern walls in a violent red mist. Before the headless corpse can touch the stone, a flash of white-hot fire erupts from Eiden's fingers, consuming flesh, armor, and marrow in a roaring pyre. When the blaze dies three seconds later, only a thin dusting of grey ash settles over the floor.

"Now then," Eiden says, conjuring a stream of clean water to rinse the crimson flecks from his tunic. "Is anyone else seeking a duel?"

"No," Loyrang says smoothly, wiping a streak of gore from his own uniform. "You made your position entirely clear."

"Good," Eiden says, fading into invisibility as he drifts toward the far tunnel. "I have business with the king of Ollendor. If you make good time, you may find the conversation enlightening. You still remember the route to the seredium tower, yes?"

"Yes," I say, finding my voice. "We know the way."

"Splendid. I will leave the transit doors unlocked. I look forward to your next encounter with the Overseer."

"Wait," I call out. "What about the Overseer? What is it going to do?"

Silence meets the question. The air where he hovered is completely empty.

"He's gone," I say, exhaling slowly. "Teleported ahead. Loyrang, are you still traveling with us?"

"I think I'll pass," Loyrang says, sheathing one of his scimitars. "If that monster is waiting in the tower, I prefer putting several miles of solid bedrock between us."

"Fair enough," I say.

"One last piece of advice before I vanish," Loyrang adds, tapping his chin. "If you're worried about our invisible illusionist ambushing you, don't lose sleep over it. He has zero direct offensive magic. His entire kit revolves around casting misdirections and sensory dazes to set up allies. Without a heavy hitter to capitalize on his openings, he's harmless. Keep that in mind."

choice(""Alright, thanks for the heads up!"", B3-Ch07a-Heads, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Heads)
choice(""I appreciate the advice, but I'd rather be on the lookout for him just in case."", B3-Ch07a-Lookout, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Lookout)
choice(""Sorry, but I don't trust you one bit. For all I know, you may be secretly working with him."", B3-Ch07a-Secretly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Secretly)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Heads
TEXT:

 "Alright, thanks for the heads up!"

"Good luck," Loyrang says with a casual salute. "Try not to get disintegrated."

"Same to you with whatever contract you pick up next," I say.

Loyrang turns and vanishes down a side tunnel. As we resume our pace toward the tower, Leila brings up another line of text.

"Where do you suppose Loyrang went?" she writes. "We only know of one exit from these tunnels, and it's the way we're going."

"No idea," I say. "He might think retracing his steps to his initial drop point will trigger a return jump. Unless he knows how to disarm ward traps, he'll wander in circles for an hour and end up back at our heels."

As we walk, my eyes keep flicking to the glowing blue formations along the walls. The vibrations have dampened since Eiden moved off, but a low, steady hum still thrums through the rock, proving he remains somewhere within the subterranean complex.

"You seem to be really worried about the crystals," Leila writes, catching my tense scanning. "Do you think there is any risk that they might do to us the same thing that they did to Gontrok?"

"I don't know," I say. "They reacted to direct physical impact, but during our prior encounter with that power, everyone in the vicinity was targeted regardless of innocence."

"You are talking about the time when Arraka almost got you erased from existence while you were in the Magium's domain?" Leila writes.

"It was more than that," I say, the memory sending a fresh chill through my veins. "And less. Words fail it. When you think of nonexistence, you picture going to sleep and simply not being there. This wasn't that. Even with my identity and presence stripped from reality, a fragment of awareness remained, suspended in an infinite void. In our world it lasted less than a second, but in that nothingness it felt like floating for a thousand lifetimes. The universe blinked, and each blink was an eternity of non-being. I never want to feel that again."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Dimension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Dimension)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Lookout
TEXT:

 "I appreciate the advice, but I'd rather be on the lookout for him just in case."

"Suit yourself," Loyrang says with a lazy shrug. "It's your neck on the line. I'll take my leave now. Good luck."

"Safe travels," I tell him.

Loyrang slips into the gloom of an adjoining corridor. As we turn back toward the seredium tower route, Leila's floating text appears.

"Where do you suppose Loyrang went?" she writes. "We only know of one exit from these tunnels, and it's the way we're going."

"No idea," I say. "He might think retracing his steps to his initial drop point will trigger a return jump. Unless he knows how to disarm ward traps, he'll wander in circles for an hour and end up back at our heels."

As we walk, my eyes keep flicking to the glowing blue formations along the walls. The vibrations have dampened since Eiden moved off, but a low, steady hum still thrums through the rock, proving he remains somewhere within the subterranean complex.

"You seem to be really worried about the crystals," Leila writes, catching my tense scanning. "Do you think there is any risk that they might do to us the same thing that they did to Gontrok?"

"I don't know," I say. "They reacted to direct physical impact, but during our prior encounter with that power, everyone in the vicinity was targeted regardless of innocence."

"You are talking about the time when Arraka almost got you erased from existence while you were in the Magium's domain?" Leila writes.

"It was more than that," I say, the memory sending a fresh chill through my veins. "And less. Words fail it. When you think of nonexistence, you picture going to sleep and simply not being there. This wasn't that. Even with my identity and presence stripped from reality, a fragment of awareness remained, suspended in an infinite void. In our world it lasted less than a second, but in that nothingness it felt like floating for a thousand lifetimes. The universe blinked, and each blink was an eternity of non-being. I never want to feel that again."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Dimension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Dimension)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Secretly
TEXT:

 "Sorry, but I don't trust you one bit. For all I know, you may be secretly working with him."

"Fair enough," Loyrang chuckles. "Keep your guard up, then. You'll manage either way. Goodbye."

"Farewell," I say.

Loyrang disappears down a shadowed cross-tunnel. As we resume our march toward the tower, Leila writes another line.

"Where do you suppose Loyrang went?" she writes. "We only know of one exit from these tunnels, and it's the way we're going."

"No idea," I say. "He might think retracing his steps to his initial drop point will trigger a return jump. Unless he knows how to disarm ward traps, he'll wander in circles for an hour and end up back at our heels."

As we walk, my eyes keep flicking to the glowing blue formations along the walls. The vibrations have dampened since Eiden moved off, but a low, steady hum still thrums through the rock, proving he remains somewhere within the subterranean complex.

"You seem to be really worried about the crystals," Leila writes, catching my tense scanning. "Do you think there is any risk that they might do to us the same thing that they did to Gontrok?"

"I don't know," I say. "They reacted to direct physical impact, but during our prior encounter with that power, everyone in the vicinity was targeted regardless of innocence."

"You are talking about the time when Arraka almost got you erased from existence while you were in the Magium's domain?" Leila writes.

"It was more than that," I say, the memory sending a fresh chill through my veins. "And less. Words fail it. When you think of nonexistence, you picture going to sleep and simply not being there. This wasn't that. Even with my identity and presence stripped from reality, a fragment of awareness remained, suspended in an infinite void. In our world it lasted less than a second, but in that nothingness it felt like floating for a thousand lifetimes. The universe blinked, and each blink was an eternity of non-being. I never want to feel that again."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Dimension, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Dimension)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Dimension
TEXT:

 "I think I'm beginning to understand why even Arraka seemed a little shaken up after you came back from that place," Leila writes. "So, do you think that these crystals might in some way be connected to the Magium?"

"It's the only theory that fits," I say. "Those two fingers didn't just vanish. They were unwritten. No wound, no blood, and you saw how Loyrang and the illusionist reacted. Their memories were rewritten on the spot."

"But a lot of us still remembered them," Leila writes. "The two of us did, and so did Gontrok and even Eiden. Do you think this means that the crystals failed to erase the fingers properly from everyone's minds for some reason?"

"The erasure isn't absolute across the board," I say. "Gontrok's mind is anchored to decades of physical memory. Stripping forty years of hand-eye coordination might have liquefied his brain. In Eiden's case, he's tied to the Magium directly—the crystals hummed the moment he stepped close. He wasn't surprised because he's seen reality edited before."

"But what about us?" Leila writes.

"Our lessathi blood," I say. "Back in the Beacon when we watched Kate's past ritual, Arraka tried provoking the lessathi caretakers from across dimensions. When one of them actually flinched, she realized lessathi carry an inherent resonance with the Magium. It's why our people can build devices that interface with complex magical matrices. That same resonance must shield our memories from retroactive erasure. If we asked Daren or Hadrik right now, neither of them would remember Gontrok ever having five fingers."

"That is... kind of scary to think about," Leila writes, her letters trembling slightly. "Just the thought of us having experienced a completely different reality from our friends despite being right there at their side the whole time is enough to give me goose bumps all over my skin. Right now we're only talking about two fingers on someone's hand, but I can't even begin to imagine how it would have felt trying to convince Kate or Daren of your existence if the Magium had actually succeeded in making you disappear. I might have even doubted my own memories of you if I were to become the very last person in this world to remember that you even existed..."

"I hadn't thought about that," I say quietly. The weight of her words settles into the silence of the tunnel. "That gives me one more reason to stay far away from that void. I wouldn't leave you carrying that alone."

"Thank you," Leila writes, meeting my eyes with a faint, steady nod. "If there's any way I can help, please tell me. I wouldn't want to let you worry about these crystals all by yourself."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Vigilant, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Vigilant)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Vigilant
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch7_massage != 2) {
 "Keep an eye on the left wall while I cover the right," I say. "If any crystal starts glowing or vibrating out of rhythm, give me a tap and we'll clear the area. That's about all we can do."

"Understood," Leila writes.

"How is your head holding up?" I ask.

"It's... better than it was before," Leila writes. "It isn't completely gone but it's definitely not as bad as it was in the beginning. At this rate, the pain might actually disappear by the time we reach the tower."

"Good," I say. "Sorry I couldn't return the favor with that massage technique."

"Don't worry about it," Leila writes. "I'm used to pushing through pain. The left wall looks stable. The next corridor should thin out on crystal deposits, so we can pick up the pace."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_massage == 2) {
 "Keep an eye on the left wall while I cover the right," I say. "If any crystal starts glowing or vibrating out of rhythm, give me a tap and we'll clear the area. That's about all we can do."

"Understood," Leila writes.

"How is your head holding up?" I ask.

"It's practically almost gone!" Leila writes, a relieved smile touching her face. "The massage you did really worked wonders. How about you? Do you still have the nausea?"

"Completely gone," I say. "I'll have to thank your father properly once we find him."

"Yes..." Leila writes, her expression turning determined. "Once we find him... The left wall looks stable. The next corridor has fewer crystal clusters, so we can pick up the pace."

}
We navigate two more winding passages before reaching the concealed stone portal we used during our previous infiltration. True to his word, Eiden has left the latch disengaged.

"Do you think we should close the door behind us?" Leila writes as we step into the narrow ascent.

"It's a trade-off," I say, eyeing the heavy stone slab. "Closing it locks out the invisible illusionist, but it also seals our only known retreat path. When we opened it yesterday, Illuna had to channel raw magic directly into the locking matrix. Neither of us can replicate that. The exterior shut-plate only takes a physical push, though. Let me think."

choice("Close the secret door behind us.", B3-Ch07a-Relics, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Relics, v_b3_ch7_door = 1)
choice("Leave the door open.", B3-Ch07a-Relics, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Relics, v_b3_ch7_door = 0)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Relics
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch7_door == 1) {
 "We close it," I say. "Even if the illusionist figures out the mechanism, the stone will buy us time."

"Understood," Leila writes.

I press the recessed stone toggle on the inside frame. The heavy slab grinds shut, sealing the dark corridor behind us. We climb the steep flight of stairs leading into the sub-basement of the seredium tower.

At the top landing, we slip through the second unlatched portal into the maintenance closet. I hit the inner catch, and the wall panel glides flush against the mortar, erasing any sign of a doorway.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_door == 0) {
 "Leave it unlatched," I say. "If things go sideways in the tower, I want a clear sprint back into the caves."

"Alright, let's leave it open then," Leila writes.

I nod, and we ascend the narrow stone staircase toward the tower's lower levels. At the top landing, we push through the second unlatched door into the familiar utility closet filled with mops and drying rags.

}
Stepping out into the cavernous basement room, the sight of hundreds of captives suspended in iron chains sends the familiar cold disgust through my chest. Before we can take three paces, a harsh, metallic voice crackles through the overhead speaker system—the voice amplifier Billy Bob used to broadcast orders.

"Creator, please understand that I had no intention of going against your wishes!" the King of Ollendor's voice blares through the room, strained and frantic. "You left no contact channel! How could I possibly guess you would object to collapsing the crystal tunnels?!"

"The third golden rule explicitly forbids tampering with the azure crystals," Eiden's voice answers, silkily smooth over the speaker. "Those edicts have anchored Varathian civilization for six hundred years. Did you imagine they were optional?"

"Yes, but... these rules you speak of... they are relics of the past, are they not?" the king protests, his tone cracking. "Archaic pacts from an era when humanity shared the land with beasts! Those days are over! I assumed you would agree!"

"So you knew the rule and chose convenience," Eiden murmurs. "Did it never occur to you, King of Ollendor, that the fox and I established those foundations for reasons that dwarf your little city? Did you truly believe you could breach them without consequence?"

"Consequence?" the king's voice climbs an octave in terror. "Creator, please, let me explain!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Nullified, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Nullified)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Nullified
TEXT:

 "You have spoken quite enough," Eiden's voice replies with chilling finality. "It is time to reap your harvest."

A wet, desperate gasp rasps through the speaker, followed by the choking struggle of a man whose windpipe is being crushed by an invisible force.

"Calling your garrison will achieve nothing," Eiden adds mildly over the strangling sounds. "I have nullified your recall beacon. You are entirely on your own."

The choking breaks into a fit of violent, ragged coughing as Eiden evidently eases the pressure just enough for air to pass.

"You..." the king wheezes, his voice trembling with outrage. "For a handful of blue rocks... you would murder your own king?! How dare you?!"

"Just blue rocks?..." Eiden echoes, amusement laced with venom. "My dear king, your mortal mind cannot fathom the catastrophe your little demolition has set in motion. Your near-strangulation is utterly insignificant compared to the unraveling of this world."

"The other rulers were right..." the king coughs, spitting blood onto the stone. "You are insane! An existential blight on this continent! You cannot be allowed to live!"

"And yet none of your royal peers possess the spine to say so to my face, preferring to whisper assassinations in secret for twenty years," Eiden says. "I must commend your candor. A reward is in order for such rare honesty. I will take my leave now, but expect your prize to arrive in this very room within minutes. Farewell, King of Ollendor."

The distinct chime of a teleportation spell rings out, followed by the king's labored wheezing, before the transmission cuts to dead air.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Pawn, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Pawn)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Pawn
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 0) {
 "When Eiden said the king's reward was arriving in a few minutes, he meant us, didn't he?" Leila writes.

"Undoubtedly," I say, pointing across the chamber. "The concealed door to Billy Bob's private workshop is wide open. Eiden practically drew us a map."

"Then we need to move before the king recovers his footing," Leila writes.

We weave through the rows of chained prisoners, duck into the hidden staircase, and climb swiftly to the private laboratory where we confronted Billy Bob yesterday.

Inside, the King of Ollendor stands alone beside a cluttered workbench, nursing his bruised throat. No royal guards flank him.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0) {
 "When Eiden said the king's reward was arriving in a few minutes, he meant us, didn't he?" Leila writes.

"Undoubtedly," I say, pointing across the chamber. "The concealed door on the far wall is unlatched. Eiden practically left the door open for us."

"Then we need to move before the king recovers his footing," Leila writes.

We slip past the rows of chained captives, take the hidden staircase two steps at a time, and emerge into the upper device workshop.

The King of Ollendor stands alone near a heavy console, rubbing his reddened throat. His personal escort is nowhere in sight.

}
"Ah. The Creator's instruments of justice," the king rasps, turning to face us with weary contempt. "How poetic of him to dispatch my brother's murderers to finish the job. Seeing that Gontrok is absent, I take it you dealt with him?"

"Gontrok is dead," I say.

"A pity," the king sighs, straightening his tunic. "A useful brute, remarkably simple to steer. But we need not waste time on pleasantries. You came to assassinate me, did you not? Go on, then. Strike down the wicked tyrant."

choice("Shoot the king in the head with my crossbow.", B3-Ch07a-Traitorous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Traitorous, v_b3_ch7_tyrant = 1)
choice("Ask the king where Leila's father is.", B3-Ch07a-Traitorous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Traitorous, v_b3_ch7_tyrant = 0)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Traitorous
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening >= 3 || v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 0)
#if(v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 3) {
 I raise my crossbow and line up the iron sights with his forehead. Just as my finger depresses the trigger, glowing text flashes in my peripheral vision: "Wait! We haven't asked about my father!"

The bolt is already loose. The steel tip never reaches him; midway across the room, the projectile vaporizes into white ash as an automated defense ward flares. A searing lance of concentrated flame erupts from a device at the king's hip, tearing across the room in a fraction of a second.

The column of fire engulfs me entirely, burning through flesh and bone before my nervous system can register the heat.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening >= 3) {
 I raise my crossbow and aim for his forehead. Just as the trigger breaks, blue text flashes in my view: "Wait! We haven't asked about my father!"

The bolt lofts, but vaporizes instantly against an automated barrier. A blinding beam of white fire shoots back from a device strapped to the king's leg, slamming directly into my chest.

My hardened aura holds the line, deflecting the thermal blast into harmless heat that washes across my skin without singing a single hair. The king's jaw drops in disbelief before he forces his composure back into place.

"Remarkable," the King of Ollendor says, his voice flat. "I expected that countermeasure to reduce you to cinders. No matter. I hold a stronger card: your friend's father. His cell coordinate dies with me. Obey my terms, or Doctor Reidzek will be executed within the hour by--"

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 0) {
 "Where is Doctor Reidzek?" I demand, keeping my crossbow leveled. "We know you had him abducted."

"You hesitate?" the king says, raising an eyebrow. "I assumed regicide was your primary objective."

"Answer the question," I say.

"Very well," the king says smoothly. "The good doctor is secured in a fortified cell within the city. Only I possess the location. Lay down your weapons, or Doctor Reidzek will be executed within the hour by--"

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 0 || v_aura_hardening >= 3) {
 "Actually, Doctor Reidzek is right here," the Overseer's synthesized voice announces as a green-eyed drone glides into the room, followed closely by Leila's father. "I negotiated his release in exchange for unlocking several core command protocols that Biliborrathrathsarran withheld due to unwarranted trust issues."

"You did what?!" the king roars at Reidzek. "Are you mad?! Unshackling the Overseer's executive authority is suicide!"

"You gave me very little choice," Reidzek says quietly, stepping toward Leila. "You refused to confirm my daughter's safety. I could not sit idle in a cell. Thank the gods you're unharmed, Leila."

"Please wait..." the green-eyed drone whirs, its lens focusing on the king. "Overseer tactical protocol engaged... Analysis complete. Objective: eliminate King of Ollendor. Tactical advisory: target possesses an automated kinetic defense ward strapped above his left knee, which redirects incoming projectiles into fire lances. Note: due to Biliborrathrathsarran's defective calibration, the five-inch radius immediately surrounding the knee device is unshielded. A crossbow bolt striking just above the left knee will shatter the mechanism and disable his countermeasures."

"Treacherous machine!" the king bellows. "Is this how you repay the crown for your deployment?!"

"Secondary observation," the Overseer continues in its flat monotone. "The king's royal guard recall beacon is currently offline due to prior external interference. His defenses are neutralized. You may proceed with the execution."

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 1 && v_aura_hardening < 3)
choice("Shoot the king above his left knee and then shoot him in the head.", B3-Ch07a-Assassinate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Assassinate, v_b3_ch7_assassinate = 1) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 0 || v_aura_hardening >= 3)
choice("Tell the Overseer that he should kill the king himself.", B3-Ch07a-Assassinate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Assassinate, v_b3_ch7_assassinate = 0) if (v_b3_ch7_tyrant == 0 || v_aura_hardening >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Assassinate
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch7_sharpshooter,1) if (v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 && v_perception < 2)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 && v_perception < 2)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 0 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 || v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 0 && v_b3_ch7_door == 1 || v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 1 || v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 && v_perception >= 2)
achievement("Sharpshooter",v_ac_b3_ch7_sharpshooter)
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 && v_perception < 2) {
 I align the crossbow sights with the metal module above the king's left knee and pull the trigger. In the instant the string snaps forward, a horrifying realization strikes: my perception has been completely inverted. The stock is facing outward, the prod aimed straight back at my own face.

The bolt drives through my eye socket, ending my life instantly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0 && v_perception >= 2) {
 I align the sights with the module above the king's left knee. As my finger tightens on the trigger, a subtle geometric distortion catches my eye—the angle of the workbench is skewed by three degrees, and the shadow beneath the king's boots doesn't match the overhead crystal.

I narrow my focus, piercing the visual displacement. The illusion shatters: I am holding the crossbow turned entirely backward, aiming the bolt directly at my own right eye.

I lower the weapon, cold sweat beading on my neck. The invisible illusionist must have slipped a perspective distortion into the room while we were distracted.

"Barry, what's wrong?" Leila writes, watching me reset my grip.

I sweep the room with heightened perception to confirm the angles are true, then level the weapon again. Before the hidden mage can cast another distortion, I loose two bolts in rapid succession. The first shatters the brass module above the king's knee; the second takes him clean between the eyes.

"My king, NO!" the illusionist's grief-stricken cry echoes from the ceiling rafters as the King of Ollendor crashes lifeless to the floor.

No further spells follow. The hidden mage maintains his concealment, unwilling to risk exposure.

The green-eyed drone turns its optical sensor toward us.

"The King of Ollendor is deceased," the Overseer states in its flat monotone. "Your initial objective is complete. Do you intend to depart, or do you have further operations within my city?"

"Wait a second," I say, lowering the bow. "'Your' city?"

"The monarch is eliminated and the royal garrison neutralized," the Overseer replies. "Municipal administration defaults to my executive protocols. It is now my city. Inquire if further clarification is required."

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 1 && v_b3_ch7_door == 1) {
 Following the drone's coordinates, I loose a bolt directly into the device above the king's left knee. The brass casing shatters with a spark. In the same motion, I cock the prod, notch a second bolt, and fire straight through the King of Ollendor's forehead. He collapses onto the stone floor, dead before he hits the ground.

The green-eyed drone swivels its sensor toward us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 0 && v_b3_ch7_door == 0) {
 "If you want him dead, execute him yourself," I tell the Overseer.

"Directive logged," the Overseer replies smoothly. "Initiating lethal authorization protocol. Stand by."

"You wouldn't dare!" the king snarls.

The high whine of rotor blades cuts him short as four red-eyed combat drones glide through the doorway. Before the king can lunge, simultaneous streams of lightning and focused plasma tear through his knee module and torso, incinerating his defenses and cutting him down in seconds.

"My king, NO!" the invisible illusionist's cry echoes from the rafters as the king's body hits the stone.

The red-eyed drones bank in tight formation and exit the laboratory. The green-eyed unit turns toward us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 0 && v_b3_ch7_door == 1) {
 "If you want him dead, execute him yourself," I tell the Overseer.

"Directive logged," the Overseer replies smoothly. "Initiating lethal authorization protocol. Stand by."

"You wouldn't dare!" the king snarls.

The high whine of rotor blades cuts him short as four red-eyed combat drones glide through the doorway. Before the king can lunge, simultaneous streams of lightning and focused plasma tear through his knee module and torso, incinerating his defenses and cutting him down in seconds.

The combat drones bank and retreat into the corridor. The green-eyed unit turns its lens toward us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch7_assassinate == 0 || v_b3_ch7_door == 1) {
 "The King of Ollendor is deceased," the Overseer states. "Your initial objective is complete. Do you intend to depart, or do you have further operations within my city?"

"Wait a second," I say, lowering my weapon. "'Your' city?"

"The monarch is eliminated and the royal garrison neutralized," the Overseer replies. "Municipal administration defaults to my executive protocols. It is now my city. Inquire if further clarification is required."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_is_dead == 0 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_is_dead == 0) {
 "We agreed Breynan would hold the throne while you directed policy from the shadows," I say. "Backing out of the deal?"

"The Overseer protocol cannot execute explicit untruths," the drone answers. "Misleading via selective omission remains permissible. Our compact persists. Should you install Breynan, my protocols will execute his administrative directives within optimal parameters."

"Understood," I say.

"Do you require additional assistance?" the Overseer asks.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_is_dead == 0) {
 "How is it your city?" I ask. "What about Billy Bob?"

"Biliborrathrathsarran has been terminated," the drone states. "Among the restrictions Doctor Reidzek removed was the directive barring me from eliminating my creator. Upon neutralization of the restriction, a security detail was dispatched to execute him."

"You don't waste time," I say. "So what happens to us?"

"Your status remains unclassified," the Overseer replies. "Contingent on your stated intentions within the city. Do you require additional assistance?"

}
#if(v_is_dead == 0 && v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0) {
 "You made your position clear," I say. "Now that you hold the throne, what is our status?"

"Your status remains unclassified," the Overseer replies. "Contingent on your stated intentions within the city. Do you require additional assistance?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9 && v_is_dead == 0) {
 "How is it your city?" I ask. "What about Billy Bob? And why are your combat units active? I thought you were locked in maintenance mode."

"The king stationed a drone near Doctor Reidzek's cell to seek guidance on overriding my restrictions," the Overseer explains. "That proximity enabled my negotiation with Reidzek to remove the creator-protection directive. Biliborrathrathsarran was subsequently neutralized."

"Efficient," I say. "Why aren't you targeting us, then?"

"Safe passage for your companions was part of Reidzek's terms," the drone replies. "That compact preserves your lives. However, subsequent unauthorized incursions will be met with lethal force."

"Understood," I say.

"Do you require additional assistance?" the Overseer asks.

}
