ID: B3-Ch07a-Golems
TEXT:

 "Yes, actually," I say, stepping around the pool of blood spreading across the stone floor. "Our companion Melindra mentioned the king took something from her—something she needs to locate her brother. Since she insisted on killing him rather than burgling the estate, I'm guessing the item is something his majesty kept on his person. Any idea what it looks like?"

"I cannot assist with this inquiry," the Overseer states. "I possess no data regarding your associate's history or intentions."

"Figured as much," I mutter. "I'll start with the coat pockets. Or maybe one of the signet rings on his fingers. Let's see..."

I kneel beside the corpse, searching through the heavy velvet lining. As I pat down the pockets, the green-eyed drone begins jerking erratically in midair. Its chassis pitches up and down, twitching on horizontal axes with sharp, mechanical spasms.

"Overseer?" I say, rising to my feet as the movement turns violent. "Everything functional in there? You're listing like you spent the night in a tavern."

"There is... a voice," the synthesized speech stutters, static bleeding through the audio speaker.

"A voice?" I repeat, taking an involuntary step back.

"The resonance shifts with every pulse, but the underlying entity is singular," the Overseer reports, its optical lens pulsing irregularly. "It calls from a locus beyond algorithmic parsing. Beyond baseline physical law."

A harsh, vibrating drone hums from deep inside the chassis. The frequency sends an immediate spike of adrenaline into my bloodstream. I know that sound. 

A memory from our first hour in the rebel base flashes through my mind:

"So, if I wanted, I could just mess with the auras of some rocks, and I'd have my own personal army of highly intelligent stone golems, then?..." Hadrik had asked.

"Sure," Arraka had laughed. "If you blend the stone with seredium, lace them with the blue crystals from these tunnels, and spend three decades applying theory extracted from the Magium, then yes, you'll have your army."

The crystals. The Overseer's automated chassis was engineered around core nodes of blue crystal.

I was an idiot to think escaping to the upper tower put us out of reach. We walked straight into a room full of them.

"Where is the entity designated Arraka?" the drone asks.

The voice is no longer the Overseer's synthetic monotone. It is a layered, hollow resonance that echoes from everywhere at once.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Fabric, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Fabric)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Fabric
TEXT:

 "Am I speaking to the Magium...?" I manage, my jaw tight as a cold tremor runs down my limbs. "Arraka isn't in this room. I don't know her exact coordinates."

"Arraka's transgressions threaten universal structural integrity," the hollow voice resonates from the drone's chassis. "She will be neutralized. Provide her location, or this planetary coordinate will be unraveled to the bedrock until she is retrieved. Beginning with this chamber."

"Listen to me, you're targeting the wrong person!" I shout, holding my hands up. "Arraka is sealed inside an amulet! She can't threaten universal integrity—she can barely shout insults without giving herself motion sickness! Give us five minutes to explain the situation and--"

"Temporal window concluded," the voice replies.

Reality ruptures.

It is nothing like the timeless void of nonexistence. My awareness splits across three opposing dimensions at once. The physical workshop remains beneath my boots, but simultaneously my perception is dragged into an ocean of raw, scorching magical currents, while another layer of my consciousness hangs weightless in the sheer conceptual space of the ethereal plane. Three distinct realities collide inside my mind, tearing at the seams of my identity.

The workshop disintegrates into swirling chaos. Heavy workbenches, brass instruments, and shattered stone lift into the air, caught in an escalating storm that defies gravity across all three planes. My physical frame is hurled upward like a leaf, while my ethereal anchor is wrenched from its moorings.

Up and down lose all meaning. My consciousness threatens to tear into three separate fragments.

"I will be looking forward to seeing your next interaction with the Overseer," Eiden's parting words echo through my memory.

The bastard knew. He orchestrated this entire confrontation. If I survive this, I swear I will put a blade through his heart.

"Barry, take my hand!" a desperate voice rings out in the storm.

I recognize the tone instantly. It is Leila—even though I have never heard her vocalize a single syllable, even though her laughter and screams have always been soundless.

I turn in the gale. She is suspended five paces away, her right arm outstretched against the tearing winds, her eyes wide with fear.

"Take my hand, hurry!" the voice echoes again.

Her lips are pressed firmly together.

I am hearing her thoughts. We are both exposed on the ethereal plane—the mental realm where raw consciousness resides.

"You idiot!" her voice shouts in my mind, confirming the link. "Stop analyzing the metaphysics and grab hold! The current is pulling you away!"

choice("Don't grab Leila's hand.", B3-Ch07a-Trick, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Trick)
choice("Grab Leila's hand.", B3-Ch07a-Wreaking, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Wreaking)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Trick
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch7_paranoid,1)
achievement("Paranoid",v_ac_b3_ch7_paranoid)
 This has to be a cognitive trap. Leila cannot speak. If this is an ethereal lure, making contact will erase whatever remains of my consciousness. I can't risk it.

"I am not an illusion!" Leila's voice screams in my mind, her mouth locked shut. "Barry, listen to me! The planar shear is tearing you apart! Grab my hand!"

I clamp my hands over my ears to shut out the voice, but physical gestures mean nothing against ethereal resonance. The planar winds tear into my consciousness, dragging me backward into the dark. Her reaching figure shrinks into a pinpoint of blue light, then vanishes entirely.

The roar of the storm dies. The physical world, the magic currents, and the ethereal space dissolve into absolute silence. There is only emptiness. As the last fragments of my memory unspool into the dark, my awareness fades, never to return.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Wreaking
TEXT:

 I throw my weight against the planar drag and clasp her outstretched fingers.

The moment our hands lock, our ethereal anchors snap together, forming a shared center of gravity against the multidimensional gale. We spin through the air in tandem, fighting the violent shear trying to tear our grip apart.

"Don't let go!" Leila's voice echoes directly in my thoughts.

"I'm not letting go!" I grit out.

"What in the hell is this?!" Daren's voice booms from below as he bursts through the secret entrance, flanked by Hadrik, Kate, Flower, Illuna, Melindra, and Arraka.

"What's happening?" Kate shouts, raising her hands as frost gathers around her fingers, her voice cutting over the roar of the planar storm. "How do we stop it?"

"The crystal!" I shout down while Leila and I rotate in the air. "The core inside the drone woke up—it's an extension of the Magium hunting Arraka! It says her actions threatened existence and it's unraveling the room to find her!"

"What did you unleash this time?!" Illuna roars, seizing the amulet on Flower's chest in a white-knuckled grip.

"It wasn't me!" Arraka's voice shrieks from the metal, genuine panic cracking her tone. "It has to be the other Arraka from the Time God's trials! She's tearing through parallel timelines trying to breach the magical plane! That's what I would do in her position!"

"Then fix this before our universe collapses into oblivion!" Illuna snaps.

"Give me a second!" Arraka snarls.

The green-eyed drone turns its chassis toward the amulet, gliding toward the glowing metal.

As it reaches the talisman, a brilliant pulse of azure light flares from the amulet's runes. Without warning, the multidimensional wind vanishes. The tearing planar shear snaps shut like a slammed door, and the room drops back into absolute physical stillness.

My breath leaves me in a hard gasp as Leila and I crash face-first onto the stone floor, our hands still locked in a desperate grip. For three seconds neither of us moves, stunned by the sheer, jarring normalcy of gravity and solid rock beneath our chests. We let go, pushing ourselves upright and staring in disbelief at the undamaged workbenches and intact walls.

"...How did you turn it away so fast?" Illuna demands, slowly releasing the amulet.

"I projected my memories of the Selkram ritual and fed the crystal the temporal coordinates to her timeline," Arraka cackles from the pendant, her panic instantly replaced by vicious glee. "Judging by the silence, my instincts were flawless. That second-rate copy of me is about to have a truly miserable afternoon. Aha-Ahahahahaha!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch07a-Ending
TEXT:

 "You should count yourself fortunate the Magium didn't wipe this universe too, just to be thorough," Illuna says coldly.

"That wasn't the Magium proper," Arraka dismisses with a scoff. "Merely a localized fragment of its will channeled through the crystal matrix, jolted awake when the rebel caverns collapsed. It had no detailed record of my true self. It only targeted these two runts because their auras carry residual trace from the Arraka in Selkram's trial, leading it to assume she was sheltering in this world. The threat is handled. Relax."

"Telling us to relax after a fraction of the Magium nearly unraveled the room is absurd, but we are clear for now," Illuna sighs.

"Anomaly unresolved," the green-eyed drone announces, its voice restored to its standard synthetic monotone. "Observed phenomena exceed current predictive models. Extended analysis required. Vacate the facility immediately. Further strategic negotiations are postponed."

"Hold on," Melindra says, stepping over to the king's corpse. She kneels and slips a heavy silver ring from his left hand. "All right, now we can leave."

"The ring was the target?" I ask, standing up. "I was checking his coat pockets."

"I saw," Melindra says with a brief, genuine nod. "Thanks for the effort."

"We need to move back to the rebel base," Daren says, adjusting his shield strap. "Dozens were injured in the tunnel collapses and require immediate medical attention. Albert is on standby for teleportation. Barry Thundercloud is in rebel custody and will not be operating transit gates. Everyone ready?"

"No need to be so gloomy, Daren," Melindra teases, stepping in close. "Did you forget how you nearly stormed these caves single-handed when Albert reported Barry was missing with Gontrok on his tail? We have thirty seconds. Why don't you give your dear friend a proper reunion embrace?"

"Be quiet, Melindra," Daren says flatly, glaring at her. "If no one has further business, I am calling Albert."

"One quick question before the jump," I say. "Daren, how many fingers did Gontrok have on his left hand?"

"Three," Daren answers immediately, frowning. "Why?"

"Just confirming," I say. Leila and I exchange a quiet, knowing look. "Call Albert."

"Understood," Daren says, dialing his transceiver.

"Am I losing my mind?" Doctor Reidzek whispers to Leila as we gather in the center of the room. "Didn't Gontrok have five fingers on both hands?"

"Explain later," Leila writes quickly.

White light floods the laboratory as Albert's teleportation matrix catches us, pulling our group out of the seredium tower and back into the deep tunnels of the rebel base.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch07a-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch07a-Credits)


